cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "dnac: a lossless DNA compressor"
message: "If you use this software in academic work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Chatzichristidis
    given-names: Timotheos
    alias: timotheos-chatzichristidis
repository-code: "https://github.com/timotheos-chatzichristidis/dnac"
url: "https://github.com/timotheos-chatzichristidis/dnac"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
version: 0.9.0
date-released: "2026-09-17"
abstract: >-
  A single-file lossless DNA compressor in C. It codes each base as two binary
  decisions and predicts them with an ensemble of finite-context models,
  substitution-tolerant forward and reverse-complement match models, a two-layer
  logistic mixer and two chained SSE/APM stages, driven by a range coder. An
  optional reference mode primes the whole model on a related sequence before
  coding the target, and that primed state can be serialised and reused.
  Version 0.9.0 adds the cue: when a match model loses its place, which is what
  an insertion or a deletion does to it, a second match is loaded at a shifted
  phase and kept permanently as a mixer input rather than replacing the first.
  An indel then costs 26.9 bits instead of 48.25, and a human chromosome coded
  against another person's assembly is 6.75% smaller (19.36% on the sequence
  the two share).
  On the sequences benchmarked it compresses below GeCo3 in both reference-free
  and reference-based modes. All results are verified by SHA-256 round-trip and
  re-derived by a registry of executable claims.
keywords:
  - DNA compression
  - lossless compression
  - context mixing
  - arithmetic coding
  - referential compression
  - match models
  - bioinformatics
  - genomics
